WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.939
These readings ask when we can tell a technology is harmful.

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:08.929
The Collingridge dilemma says we often find out only after it is entrenched,

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:10.440
when change is hardest.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.149
Collingridge described two problems: we cannot predict impacts early,

00:00:15.149 --> 00:00:18.426
and we cannot easily control a technology later.

00:00:18.426 --> 00:00:21.361
His answer was intelligent trial and error,

00:00:21.361 --> 00:00:24.160
with small steps that keep learning fast.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.251
The DDT story shows a late harm being found.

00:00:27.251 --> 00:00:31.255
Early toxicity tests on adult birds showed no clear link,

00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:35.120
so the real damage to reproduction was missed at first.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.540
Researchers eventually traced the decline to eggshell thinning.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.679
DDT was banned from most uses in the United States in 1972,

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:48.240
and bird populations slowly recovered, which confirmed the cause.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.492
Social media is a harm still being argued over.

00:00:51.492 --> 00:00:53.914
Jonathan Haidt sees a direct cause.

00:00:53.914 --> 00:00:59.449
Candice Odgers says most studies show only correlation and cannot separate cause

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:00.280
from effect.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.398
Even so, the two scholars agree on some things.

00:01:03.398 --> 00:01:06.648
Tech companies should protect young users better,

00:01:06.648 --> 00:01:11.160
and more research is needed on social media and youth mental health.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.785
The Wingspread Statement offers one answer, the precautionary principle.

00:01:15.785 --> 00:01:19.640
Precautionary steps should be taken even without full proof,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.560
and the proponent of an activity carries the burden of proof.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.984
AI companions may be an earlier case.

00:01:25.984 --> 00:01:30.569
The Schwartz Reisman Institute argues the policy window is still open,

00:01:30.569 --> 00:01:35.680
so governments can set guardrails before these systems become deeply embedded.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.886
The takeaway: harms are easiest to prevent before a technology is entrenched.

00:01:40.886 --> 00:01:44.876
AI companions carry many of the same risks as social media,

00:01:44.876 --> 00:01:47.040
so the choice may still be ours.
